  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 130 PM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A typical late summer weather pattern continues \ntoday, with highs in the 90s and isolated showers and thunderstorms \npossible. \n\nThere has been little overall change from this morning for the \nforecast into tonight across central NC. Heat indices this afternoon \nwill range from the upper 90s NW to lower 100s SE. The 12z high-res \nmodels continue to indicate isolated to scattered storm activity \nthis afternoon and evening, although model scenarios differ on \ncoverage and intensity. The morning surface analysis showed a weak \nPiedmont trough along and east of the US-1 corridor. Meanwhile, the \nlatest satellite and radar trends would suggest the best coverage \nfor storms across the Sandhills into the central Coastal Plain. This \narea has seen an increase in towering cumulus, as well as lightning \nprobabilities from LightningCast. The MLCAPE in this area also \napproaches 3500 J/kg, where precipitable water values are over 2 \ninches. While we are not outlooked from SPC or WPC, given the \nenvironment and 20-25 kts of deep-layer shear, cannot rule out a \nstray downburst or two, as well as localized heavy rainfall. A few \npockets of isolated storms will be possible also in the Piedmont and \nnorthern Coastal Plain, but confidence on coverage is quite low at \nthe moment. Some CAMs suggest isolated activity tonight moving south \nout of VA (tied to development presently in OH/WV), but most of this \nshould weaken with southward extent owing to stabilization overnight.\n\nTues through Thurs: The southern flank of a belt of enhanced west-\nnorthwest mid-lvl flow over the Great Lakes and northern Mid-\nAtlantic will slowly shift south and into the Carolinas as early as \nTues and persist over the region into the late week. During this \ntime, a slight increase to the overall synoptic deep-layer shear is \nexpected (15-30 kts, greatest towards the VA border). Additionally, \nany convectively induced perturbations and remnant MCV's from more \nvigorous convection from the OH and TN Valley's may provide locally \nmore organized and greater coverage of convection along their track \nduring this time. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Risk for hot temperatures and dangerous heat begin \nto increase through late week.\n\nSub-tropical ridging elongated across FL and into the northeast Gulf \nthrough Tues is expected to build over the lower/mid MS Valley \nmidweek before shifting into the TN Valley and Deep South late week \ninto the weekend.\n\nWhile the prevailing wnwly flow pattern aloft will be favorable for \nMCS development around the sub-tropical ridge, mesoscale \nuncertainties with their timing and track will have implications on \nboth precipitation and wind potential into cntl NC and also impacts \nto what will otherwise be a hot and seasonably humid environment \nover cntl NC. Despite those uncertainties, the CAMs to varying \ndegrees depict at least one vigorous MCS moving across the Virginias \nand into at least nrn NC on Tue. Its related outflow and debris \ncloudiness would then have ramifications on the track and \ndevelopment of subsequent convective clusters into the Carolinas on \nWed. \n\nIt will otherwise be hot for most of the week, between bouts of \nconvection/outflow/cloudiness, with temperatures well into the 90s \nand with heat index values in the upr 90s to near 100 over the nw \nPiedmont to around 105 elsewhere, through at least Thu and \npotentially into Fri towards the SC border. The approach and \npossible passage of a frontal zone will probably yield less hot \ntemperatures by Sat.",
